WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.317 --> 00:00:03.650
BRANDON KVARDE: Hello, everyone.

00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:06.890
Welcome to "CE Chat,"
hosted by Cloud OnAir--

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.030
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.580
We're hosting webinars
every Tuesday.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:12.710
My name is Brandon Kvarde.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.050
I'm a Cloud customer
engineer at Google.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.070
And joining me today
is Morgan McLean,

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.080
who is a product manager
here at Google, as well.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.450
Today, we're going to be
talking about OpenCensus.

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.267
Just a quick reminder
for everybody

00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:26.600
who's watching the stream live--

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:29.130
you can ask questions
anytime on the platform.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.270
And we have Googlers on standby
to answer those questions

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:33.030
for you.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.494
So let's go ahead
and get started.

00:00:34.494 --> 00:00:35.660
MORGAN MCLEAN: Hi, everyone.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.390
So as Brandon mentioned,
my name is Morgan McLean.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.000
I'm the product manager
for OpenCensus, which is

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.410
what we're talking about today.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.080
I'm also the product manager
for our Stackdriver APM

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:49.252
line, which includes Stackdriver
Trace, Debugger, and Profiler.

00:00:49.252 --> 00:00:51.210
So let's get started,
and dive into OpenCensus.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.310
BRANDON KVARDE: Let's do it.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.230
MORGAN MCLEAN: So for
those who don't know,

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.400
OpenCensus is a set of
telemetry collection libraries.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.234
We'll get into what exactly
that means in a minute.

00:01:01.234 --> 00:01:03.650
But to start, I just want to
talk about the journey of how

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.000
we arrived at this.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.850
So inside of Google,
we use technologies

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.445
like distributed tracing,
and application metrics,

00:01:10.445 --> 00:01:12.120
and distributed metrics.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.820
And one of the challenges
that we perceived at Google

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.390
a number of years ago was
actually collecting telemetry

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.749
from our applications.

00:01:18.749 --> 00:01:21.290
So in order to capture things
like a distributed trace, which

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.260
tracks a request as it
propagates across services,

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.090
you have to have a
bunch of integrations

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.822
into all of the
network interactions

00:01:27.822 --> 00:01:29.030
that you have on your system.

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:30.210
And this is difficult.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.580
So we created a
project called Census.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.467
OpenCensus is effectively
an externalization of this.

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:37.550
And this is part of a
longer-term trend at Google.

00:01:37.550 --> 00:01:39.210
If you look at
Kubernetes, for example,

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.820
it's effectively
an externalization

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.330
of Borg, which is the
container management system.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.980
gRPC is an externalization
of something internal

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.930
called Stubby, which does RPCs.

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.115
OpenCensus is just another
part of that same story.

00:01:50.115 --> 00:01:52.490
And so we're hoping to share
the same benefits that we've

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:55.340
received at Google, from
Census, with our customers

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.690
and with the world
through OpenCensus.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.870
So let's dive into your
traditional application

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:03.700
as they were developed
a few years ago.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.984
So most things were
built as a monolith.

00:02:05.984 --> 00:02:07.900
This can be anything
from a client application

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:11.800
on a phone, or even basic
web services back in the day

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.860
where you would go and build
a single large, self-contained

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.220
application.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:19.111
These are fairly easy to get
observability and telemetry

00:02:19.111 --> 00:02:19.610
into.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.150
This has been perfected
over a number of years.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.760
Where if you want to understand
how the parts of your app

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:26.830
interact with each other,
you can do things like

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:28.630
collect a stack trace
from an exception

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:30.010
or from a break point.

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.620
And testing and staging, or
non-production environments,

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:34.300
is also fairly
straightforward with

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.980
these monolithic applications.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.480
There's a lot of
good solutions there.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.382
But as most of our viewers
are more than aware,

00:02:41.382 --> 00:02:43.090
everyone's been shifting
to microservices

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:44.090
over the last few years.

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.298
And this is for good reasons.

00:02:45.298 --> 00:02:47.460
Microservices are much
easier to operate at scale.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.010
They're much more
maintainable when

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.620
you have to build a
larger application that

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:54.200
handles hundreds,
thousands, tens of thousands

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.060
of requests per second.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.820
And so microservices are
generally really good

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:00.260
and really powerful
architecture.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.470
But one of the challenges
of microservices

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.970
is actually being able to manage
them and gain observability

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:06.330
into what's happening.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.360
So a lot of those lessons
or those techniques

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:13.730
that we had in the past where
we try and capture a trace,

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:15.500
like a stack trace
of our application,

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:17.150
are no longer really applicable.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.910
If we have the application
that you see here with services

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.460
A, B, and C, and you
want to understand

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.010
for a particular request or a
particular customer interaction

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:27.770
how those services interact,
capturing a stack trace

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:29.630
from service C
isn't that valuable

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:31.921
because you don't know what
happened in the client or A

00:03:31.921 --> 00:03:34.520
and B. And so techniques
like distributed tracing

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.690
had to be invented for that.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.450
Distributed tracing
was certainly

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:39.447
popularized by Google.

00:03:39.447 --> 00:03:41.030
We published a paper
back in, I think,

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:43.130
2010 about the Dapper
tracing system that

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.969
exists inside of a company.

00:03:44.969 --> 00:03:47.510
And what it allows you to do is
understand how these requests

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:49.020
flow across systems.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.320
So for example, if you have
some kind of interaction

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.150
you're curious
about in service C,

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:57.980
you can capture a single
customer request that

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.180
stems from your client app.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.804
This goes to service A,
does some processing there.

00:04:01.804 --> 00:04:03.720
Goes to service B, does
some processing there.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.182
It maybe calls up to service
C in database and parallel.

00:04:06.182 --> 00:04:07.640
And the distributed
trace will show

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.389
you that across all
of these applications.

00:04:09.389 --> 00:04:10.790
BRANDON KVARDE: Awesome.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.539
MORGAN MCLEAN: Another
really useful thing

00:04:12.539 --> 00:04:14.750
that's difficult to do
is distributed metrics.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.149
So for a monolith, all
we would need to do

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:19.910
is capture things like latency
metrics for the entire app.

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:22.012
And that's relatively
straightforward.

00:04:22.012 --> 00:04:23.720
But in a microservices
world, we actually

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:25.640
want to capture, for
example, latency metrics

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.470
from each individual service.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.300
It's not enough to
understand that one

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.130
of the endpoints
exposed by service A

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.154
is slow in this example.

00:04:32.154 --> 00:04:34.820
We also want to understand which
interactions on service B and C

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:35.660
are slow--

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:37.868
and so not just understanding
that B and C themselves

00:04:37.868 --> 00:04:40.790
are slow, but of the actual
end points exposed by B and C,

00:04:40.790 --> 00:04:44.960
we want to see latency and
statistics about the RPCs.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.392
And so there's a lot
of complexity here.

00:04:48.392 --> 00:04:49.850
To get this kind
of instrumentation

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:52.137
out of your app, you
have to go and build it.

00:04:52.137 --> 00:04:53.720
So this involves
getting integrations.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:54.475
And if you want
it automatically,

00:04:54.475 --> 00:04:57.280
it involves getting integrations
into various web frameworks,

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.650
RPC frameworks, storage clients,
and other client libraries

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:02.300
that your application uses.

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:03.759
And this is annoying
and difficult.

00:05:03.759 --> 00:05:06.216
And this is one of the reasons
that things like distributed

00:05:06.216 --> 00:05:08.600
tracing historically haven't
been that popular in smaller

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:09.350
companies.

00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:11.780
It's just too
expensive to get going.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.330
And so with OpenCensus,
we're making

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:15.590
this a lot more accessible.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:20.240
So with OpenCensus, we give
you automatic instrumentation.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.460
We give you APIs and
exporters to handle your data.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:24.540
And I'll dive into what
these mean in a minute.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.750
But at its core,
OpenCensus is a set

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.540
of language-specific
instrumentation libraries.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:33.230
So we have libraries
for Java, Node.js.

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:34.640
We're building one for .Net.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:39.380
We also have Go, C++,
Python, Ruby, and PHP.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.170
And so we have a lot of coverage
across all of these languages.

00:05:42.170 --> 00:05:43.628
And we have libraries
that you just

00:05:43.628 --> 00:05:46.040
link to your application
to get the observability

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.475
and insight into your app.

00:05:48.475 --> 00:05:50.850
Each of these libraries, once
linked to your application,

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:54.270
will automatically collect
traces and RPC-related metrics

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:55.720
from your application.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.920
So for example, we have
integrations for various HTTP

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.735
clients, for gRPC, for
things like MongoDB

00:06:01.735 --> 00:06:03.210
where, as a
developer, once you've

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.730
linked the library to your
app, you do no additional work.

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:07.813
We automatically will
capture traces and propagate

00:06:07.813 --> 00:06:09.360
contexts and things like that.

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:10.985
For those who aren't
aware, just when I

00:06:10.985 --> 00:06:13.080
say context propagation,
what that means is,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.090
to capture that request
that goes from A to B

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:19.012
to C into the database, you need
to pass some kind of identifier

00:06:19.012 --> 00:06:20.970
or something so that you
can actually correlate

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:23.520
all of those requests later.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.630
The next thing that
OpenCensus provides is APIs.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.130
So not only do we automatically
collect a lot of information

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:30.650
from your application,
we also let you manually

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:31.980
interact with that.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:33.890
So with the APIs,
you can do things

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:36.020
like define a custom
stat or custom metric

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:37.700
and send that to your back end.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:40.409
You can manually add spans or
components to your traces--

00:06:40.409 --> 00:06:42.200
things that weren't
automatically captured,

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:44.240
or long-running
processes that you

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:46.220
want to capture in as
a part of the trace.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:48.830
And you can add custom
metadata or custom tags

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:50.930
to both of these.

00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:53.200
You can also configure
things with the APIs.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.470
So traces that I mentioned
before, distributed traces,

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:57.880
we don't capture every request.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:06:59.840
Generally, with most
tracing systems,

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.240
there's something
called sampling

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.700
where, roughly, on
average, 1% of the requests

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:04.540
are actually traced.

00:07:04.540 --> 00:07:06.623
This is to reduce costs
and reduce the performance

00:07:06.623 --> 00:07:07.900
impact of tracing.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:11.390
The APIs in OpenCensus allow
you to configure this manually.

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:15.500
Finally, another key part
of OpenCensus is exporters.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.920
So rather than just sending
data back to Stackdriver or back

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.080
to a particular
back end, OpenCensus

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:22.974
sends data to your
back end of choice.

00:07:22.974 --> 00:07:25.140
This is why OpenCensus is
not just a Google project.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.080
We're actually participating
with a number of vendors,

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.190
though we were certainly
the initiators of it.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.220
So with OpenCensus,
you can send data

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.950
back to Zipkin or
Prometheus, which

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:36.120
are the main open-source
back ends that we target.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:39.140
So all features for OpenCensus
have to work in Prometheus.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.940
You can also send data back
to Stackdriver, of course.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.000
But you can send
your telemetry back

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.357
to Datadog, SolarWinds, Jaeger,
and a number of other back

00:07:48.357 --> 00:07:49.190
ends for processing.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:50.605
BRANDON KVARDE: Could
you send them to multiple

00:07:50.605 --> 00:07:51.650
at the same time?

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:52.190
MORGAN MCLEAN: You can.

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:53.240
And we'll go into
that in a minute--

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:54.240
so the benefits of that.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:54.755
But you can.

00:07:54.755 --> 00:07:57.800
A really common use
case for OpenCensus

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.860
is sending data to Zipkin
for tracing and Prometheus

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:01.680
for stats.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:02.180
Right?

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:06.020
And so you can send stats to one
back end and traces to another.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:08.900
You can also just send that to
multiple even of the same type.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:09.350
BRANDON KVARDE: Awesome.

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:10.580
MORGAN MCLEAN: We'll go
into that in a minute.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:11.955
But yeah, that's
a good question.

00:08:11.955 --> 00:08:14.790
It's a really powerful feature.

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:17.630
So as I mentioned before,
we have really great support

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.300
for Java, C++, and Go.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:24.080
OpenCensus has already
had tracing support--

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.726
not stat support,
but tracing support

00:08:25.726 --> 00:08:27.350
for Ruby, Python,
and PHP, though we're

00:08:27.350 --> 00:08:28.580
adding stats to those now.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:31.760
I think stats was just
added to Python last week.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:33.750
So I should have
updated this deck.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:36.710
And finally, we have full
support under development

00:08:36.710 --> 00:08:37.909
for Node.js and .Net.

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:40.130
The Node.js client also
just went into to alpha.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:44.049
So you can go use it, it's
just in a very early state.

00:08:44.049 --> 00:08:46.330
Of course, OpenCensus has
a ton of integrations.

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:48.550
There's far too many to
list, but we effectively

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:52.210
cover all HTTP,
gRPC, SQL clients,

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:55.180
storage clients like MongoDB,
and various web frameworks.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:56.890
The web framework
integrations are there

00:08:56.890 --> 00:08:59.442
to get you automatic
context propagation,

00:08:59.442 --> 00:09:01.900
so you don't have to manually
correlate an incoming request

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.440
with an outgoing one in
order to get a trace.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:05.190
It sounds trivial.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:07.523
That's actually a really
powerful feature of OpenCensus,

00:09:07.523 --> 00:09:09.640
is just removing that
work that you have to do.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:12.400
Finally, we have many exporters.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:15.400
So of course, our native
open-source exporters

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:17.620
are for Zipkin and Prometheus.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:20.860
But we also support Jaeger,
SignalFx, Datadog, SolarWinds,

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:24.410
Instana, Amazon X-Ray, and
of course, Stackdriver.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:26.800
And as you asked, you can
use multiple of these.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.633
You can mix and match
and use different ones

00:09:28.633 --> 00:09:31.550
at the same time,
which is really nice.

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:34.510
So we're going to do
a demo of OpenCensus.

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:37.010
I'm going to show you both the
telemetry that we've captured

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:39.267
and how easy it was
to get configured.

00:09:39.267 --> 00:09:41.600
The demo app for this is going
to be Hipster Shop, which

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:42.683
I'll show you in a minute.

00:09:42.683 --> 00:09:44.870
If you tuned into
Next, basically any

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:47.390
of the presentations at
Next, they used Hipster Shop.

00:09:47.390 --> 00:09:50.270
It's a new demo app that
we've created for GCP.

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:52.130
And it's an e-commerce
system that's

00:09:52.130 --> 00:09:54.290
deployed across a number
of different microservices

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:55.460
and Kubernetes.

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:57.290
So it's got a front
end written in Go,

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:00.110
and then it has several back
end services written in Python,

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:02.840
Node.js, Go, and .Net Core.

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:05.090
As I mentioned before, it's
all running on Kubernetes.

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:09.050
And it uses a combination
of HTTP and gRPC

00:10:09.050 --> 00:10:11.005
to communicate between
these services.

00:10:11.005 --> 00:10:13.130
So the front end will talk
to web browsers in HTTP,

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:15.650
obviously, but the service
interactions inside

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:18.320
of the application are
both in HTTP and gRPC.

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.237
And so this is a great
testbed for OpenCensus,

00:10:20.237 --> 00:10:22.528
because it has to do with
things like propagate context

00:10:22.528 --> 00:10:25.310
and capture traces across those
different RPC mechanisms, which

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:27.540
is traditionally not that easy.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.750
It also uses various
GCP client libraries,

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:32.360
and it can track
requests across those.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.340
This particular
demonstration of Hipster Shop

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:36.440
doesn't use an
external database.

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:39.050
But rest assured, we do support
basically all SQL clients

00:10:39.050 --> 00:10:41.133
and things like MongoDB
and other storage clients.

00:10:43.309 --> 00:10:44.350
So let's hop to the demo.

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.570
So this is the Hipster Shop app.

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:53.550
Again, if you watched
GCP Next at all,

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:55.530
you're probably intimately
familiar with it.

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:57.750
This is the slow
version of Hipster Shop

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.500
that we used in
one of the keynotes

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:02.400
when we used the Stackdriver
APM tools to diagnose it.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:04.530
We're using it for this
demonstration of OpenCensus

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:06.840
because, as a part
of being slow,

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.990
it generates really nice
telemetry for us to inspect.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:12.260
So to start, we're
going to look at traces.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:13.820
So this is Stackdriver trace.

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:16.280
Again, with OpenCensus, you
can use any tracing back end,

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:18.571
like Zipkin or others.

00:11:18.571 --> 00:11:20.570
And while we're using
Stackdriver for this demo,

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:23.149
it's just because that's
what I'm most familiar with.

00:11:23.149 --> 00:11:25.190
So what you can see here
are the various requests

00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:27.170
that were captured
from the application.

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:29.336
And all of our telemetry
in the app uses OpenCensus.

00:11:29.336 --> 00:11:31.549
And we can click
between a few of these.

00:11:31.549 --> 00:11:33.090
And so when we look
at one of these--

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:35.460
so this is a request that a
web browser made to the cart

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:36.580
service--

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:38.272
we can see how that
request was handled

00:11:38.272 --> 00:11:39.480
across all of these services.

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:42.050
And so if you weren't already
aware of what distributed

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:45.040
tracing is, and my description
earlier wasn't visual enough,

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:47.110
this should really show
you the power of it.

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:50.722
So we can see from here that
the request comes into cart,

00:11:50.722 --> 00:11:52.180
but then it has
child requests that

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:55.030
go to the currency service,
to the recommendation

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:58.270
service, and then several times
to the product catalog service.

00:11:58.270 --> 00:12:01.270
And then later on, again, it
hops to the currency service.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:04.310
So with this distributed
trace, we learn a few things.

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:06.490
First, we understand just
a few of the services

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:07.910
and how they interact.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:10.560
For example, we know that the
cart service calls the currency

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:11.840
service directly.

00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:13.887
And if we weren't developers
of this application,

00:12:13.887 --> 00:12:15.970
we didn't have intimate
knowledge of how it works,

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:17.710
the trace is a really
powerful way for us

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:19.150
to understand
those interactions.

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:22.110
Without distributed tracing,
you effectively can't see those.

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:23.680
And the next thing
we can understand

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:26.200
is how these are chained.

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:29.800
So we can see that the cart
calls the product catalog

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:32.494
service, which then the
request goes back to the cart,

00:12:32.494 --> 00:12:34.660
and then it calls the product
catalog service again.

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:36.576
And then it calls the
products catalog service

00:12:36.576 --> 00:12:37.480
again, and so on.

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:40.060
And so if we're trying to
optimize their application,

00:12:40.060 --> 00:12:42.490
this is probably something
we would want to fix.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:44.260
This seems really
inefficient when

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:47.080
we're reading a single piece
of information from the cart.

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:49.809
We might expect, maybe, the
cart has a few items in it,

00:12:49.809 --> 00:12:51.850
but it seems odd that
these calls aren't batched,

00:12:51.850 --> 00:12:53.530
or at least done in parallel.

00:12:53.530 --> 00:12:55.776
And so from just glancing
at this distributed trace,

00:12:55.776 --> 00:12:57.400
we've seen a whole
bunch of ways we can

00:12:57.400 --> 00:12:59.020
improve the latency of our app.

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:01.030
And of course, this should
be a priority for us.

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:03.071
This request to the cart
took about eight seconds

00:13:03.071 --> 00:13:05.452
to process, which is awful
for an e-commerce system.

00:13:05.452 --> 00:13:07.660
BRANDON KVARDE: So Morgan,
what we're looking at here

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:10.959
is actually a trace from a
bunch of different microservices

00:13:10.959 --> 00:13:12.250
written in different languages.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:13.930
MORGAN MCLEAN:
That's correct, yeah.

00:13:13.930 --> 00:13:16.457
And so, again, this is part
of the power of OpenCensus

00:13:16.457 --> 00:13:18.040
is the support of
all these languages.

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:18.850
BRANDON KVARDE: Awesome.

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:20.140
MORGAN MCLEAN: That's
not necessarily something

00:13:20.140 --> 00:13:21.700
you'd get with all
third-party vendors.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:23.260
Some have really great
support, some only focus

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:24.051
on a few languages.

00:13:24.051 --> 00:13:25.866
But with OpenCensus,
we've got coverage

00:13:25.866 --> 00:13:28.240
across eight of the most used
languages on the web, which

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:28.840
is really great.

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:29.923
BRANDON KVARDE: Very cool.

00:13:29.923 --> 00:13:30.820
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:33.376
And again, just to
reiterate, because OpenCensus

00:13:33.376 --> 00:13:34.750
is an open source
project-- we're

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:36.166
showing this in
Stackdriver trace.

00:13:36.166 --> 00:13:38.915
If you were using Zipkin or
a Datadog's APM software,

00:13:38.915 --> 00:13:41.990
or something else, you'd
see a very similar view.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:44.402
So now I'm going to show
you how we got this.

00:13:44.402 --> 00:13:45.860
So we have a function
that we wrote

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:47.630
in our code called
initTracing, just so we

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:48.838
could encapsulate everything.

00:13:48.838 --> 00:13:51.530
And inside of initTracing,
you can see where we actually

00:13:51.530 --> 00:13:52.640
registered OpenCensus.

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:54.681
We have one line at the
top of this app that just

00:13:54.681 --> 00:13:56.390
imports the OpenCensus library.

00:13:56.390 --> 00:14:00.680
What we do here is
actually, for a trace code,

00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:02.330
we register an exporter.

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:05.000
And then we just turn on
OpenCensus for tracing.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:05.729
And that's it.

00:14:05.729 --> 00:14:08.270
I think there's one line down
below where we had to just make

00:14:08.270 --> 00:14:10.420
a change to gRPC to
enable OpenCensus,

00:14:10.420 --> 00:14:12.230
but that's a three-line change.

00:14:12.230 --> 00:14:14.160
You've got distributed tracing.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:14.660
Go ahead.

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:15.860
BRANDON KVARDE: So
there's no tricks here.

00:14:15.860 --> 00:14:17.660
It's just this function,
and imports at the top.

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:18.170
MORGAN MCLEAN: Correct.

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:18.669
Correct.

00:14:18.669 --> 00:14:20.455
Yeah, it's really,
really straightforward.

00:14:20.455 --> 00:14:22.580
And with this, not only
are we capturing the traces

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:25.040
and sampling, but
we're also doing things

00:14:25.040 --> 00:14:27.110
like propagating
context automatically.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:29.060
So as a developer with
this particular app--

00:14:29.060 --> 00:14:30.222
it's written in Go--

00:14:30.222 --> 00:14:32.180
it's really, really simple
and straightforward.

00:14:32.180 --> 00:14:33.846
And that's the
experience that we strive

00:14:33.846 --> 00:14:35.330
for across all languages.

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:37.250
It's generally something
that we've achieved.

00:14:37.250 --> 00:14:38.340
If you're using
something like Java

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:39.780
and crossing thread
boundaries, you

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.321
do need to add a
small amount of code

00:14:41.321 --> 00:14:43.100
every time you do that
so that we can just

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:44.841
track how to propagate context.

00:14:44.841 --> 00:14:47.090
But in general, as a developer,
the work required here

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:48.756
is very, very minimal,
which is awesome.

00:14:48.756 --> 00:14:51.920
That's the core
value of OpenCensus.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.810
And with that, we were
able to capture traces

00:14:53.810 --> 00:14:55.091
across our application.

00:14:55.091 --> 00:14:57.340
Obviously, our application
has some non-Go components.

00:14:57.340 --> 00:14:58.340
So it's got Python.

00:14:58.340 --> 00:14:59.877
For that, we use
OpenCensus Python.

00:14:59.877 --> 00:15:02.210
Similar experience-- you just
import it, instantiate it.

00:15:02.210 --> 00:15:02.690
You're done.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:03.340
BRANDON KVARDE: Makes sense.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:04.173
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:15:04.173 --> 00:15:05.350
And so it's great.

00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:07.130
And because it's
just a library, you

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:09.330
can manage this entirely
through source control.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:11.741
So there's no black
box agent that

00:15:11.741 --> 00:15:13.490
needs to always update
itself or something

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:16.072
you need to set up on your
VM images or in Kubernetes.

00:15:16.072 --> 00:15:17.780
You simply link this
to your source code,

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:18.650
make a few changes.

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:20.233
You can version it,
you can control it

00:15:20.233 --> 00:15:22.041
from there, which is great.

00:15:22.041 --> 00:15:24.040
The next thing I'm going
to show you is metrics.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:25.750
So we've talked a
lot about tracing.

00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:29.240
But OpenCensus, of
course, also does stats.

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:31.720
And so I'm going to show you,
first, some of the metrics

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:34.400
that we automatically
capture from the application.

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:37.400
So as I mentioned early
on in this presentation,

00:15:37.400 --> 00:15:40.632
one of the things that's really,
really useful in microservices

00:15:40.632 --> 00:15:42.340
is understanding the
latency of every end

00:15:42.340 --> 00:15:43.720
point on every service.

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:45.520
This is not always
an easy thing to do--

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:48.040
unless you use OpenCensus,
in which case it is.

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:51.250
And so what we've got here is
a stat that's automatically

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:52.344
captured by OpenCensus.

00:15:52.344 --> 00:15:54.760
This is not a stat I had to
define and code as a developer

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:56.330
or really do anything for.

00:15:56.330 --> 00:16:00.077
And we're capturing the gRPC
latency for each end point.

00:16:00.077 --> 00:16:01.660
And so you can see
here, here's a list

00:16:01.660 --> 00:16:04.930
of endpoints for the service
that I've instrumented.

00:16:04.930 --> 00:16:06.879
And you can see the
99th percentile latency

00:16:06.879 --> 00:16:08.170
that's captured in Stackdriver.

00:16:08.170 --> 00:16:09.795
You would get the
exact same experience

00:16:09.795 --> 00:16:12.020
in Prometheus or Datadog,
or any of the other back

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:13.360
ends that OpenCensus supports.

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:14.901
Again, we're just
showing Stackdriver

00:16:14.901 --> 00:16:17.160
because it's what I happen
to be most familiar with.

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:20.060
And so we can use this to
understand that the PlaceOrder

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:22.790
method, for example,
is relatively slow

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:25.880
and has a lot of variance in
its 99th percentile latency.

00:16:25.880 --> 00:16:28.700
At the same time, the GetProduct
method on the product catalog

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:32.240
service is also slow, but
looks like its 99th percentile

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:35.570
latency is roughly
around 4 seconds.

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:37.490
But it has a lot less variance.

00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:39.614
We can also see, though,
that some parts of our app

00:16:39.614 --> 00:16:40.670
are relatively fast--

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:43.070
the GetQuote for the
shipping service,

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:45.370
or ListProducts from the
product catalog service.

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:48.720
And so if we're trying to track
the health of our application,

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:50.750
this can actually narrow
down our investigations

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:52.977
if there's a problem into
a particular end point--

00:16:52.977 --> 00:16:54.810
so not just telling us
that our app is slow,

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:57.170
not just telling us that a
certain interaction is slow,

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:59.253
but a particular end point
of a particular service

00:16:59.253 --> 00:17:00.310
is where the slowdown is.

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:01.810
And so this is great
for monitoring.

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:04.700
You can set alerts and things
like this on these metrics.

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:06.755
It's also great during
those investigations.

00:17:06.755 --> 00:17:09.130
And this is not the only metric
we capture automatically.

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:12.045
I think we capture others like
the request size and others.

00:17:12.045 --> 00:17:13.420
Just latency is
generally the one

00:17:13.420 --> 00:17:16.552
that we like to
demonstrate the most.

00:17:16.552 --> 00:17:19.995
And to capture this, if
we jump back to the code,

00:17:19.995 --> 00:17:21.119
this is what I did up here.

00:17:21.119 --> 00:17:24.050
So in addition to registering
OpenCensus for tracing,

00:17:24.050 --> 00:17:29.250
I also registered it for
our default gRPC views.

00:17:29.250 --> 00:17:32.606
So this just captures some
basic gRPC-related stats.

00:17:32.606 --> 00:17:34.730
And I actually did the
instrumentation of this part

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:35.840
of the code myself.

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:38.072
This was my first
time writing Go, ever.

00:17:38.072 --> 00:17:40.280
I do a fair amount of
programming in other languages,

00:17:40.280 --> 00:17:42.360
just haven't done it
in Go much before.

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:45.110
And I got this and
some other work done

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:46.360
in about just under one hour.

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:48.610
And someone who doesn't even
know the language working

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:53.605
with adding some instrumentation
would normally take forever,

00:17:53.605 --> 00:17:55.730
because you have to understand
what you're actually

00:17:55.730 --> 00:17:56.600
interacting with.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:57.980
For me, it was really,
really straightforward.

00:17:57.980 --> 00:17:59.030
BRANDON KVARDE: Right.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:00.110
MORGAN MCLEAN: And
so it was great.

00:18:00.110 --> 00:18:01.901
BRANDON KVARDE: And
it's that same function

00:18:01.901 --> 00:18:05.487
that we looked at earlier for
instantiating both the tracing

00:18:05.487 --> 00:18:06.320
and metric function.

00:18:06.320 --> 00:18:06.900
MORGAN MCLEAN: Correct.

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:07.490
Yeah.

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:10.310
So in the OpenCensus
parlance, we call metrics--

00:18:10.310 --> 00:18:12.380
views is one way to
describe a metric.

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:15.560
And so for this, we just write
a certain export for a view,

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:19.606
and then just told it where to
go and subscribe to metrics.

00:18:19.606 --> 00:18:22.400
There's certainly slightly
more work involved

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:23.700
in getting the view going.

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.450
You just have a few
lines of code up above

00:18:25.450 --> 00:18:29.070
to actually go and set
things up for a custom view.

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:31.220
But for any of the
built-in default views,

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:33.579
it's really, really
straightforward.

00:18:33.579 --> 00:18:35.120
Speaking of custom
views, that's what

00:18:35.120 --> 00:18:36.244
I'm going to show you next.

00:18:36.244 --> 00:18:38.030
So we also have a
custom stat that we

00:18:38.030 --> 00:18:40.970
capture from our
application with OpenCensus.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:42.550
As a developer,
there's a huge amount

00:18:42.550 --> 00:18:44.300
of value in automatically
capturing things

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:46.850
like latency metrics,
but we can also

00:18:46.850 --> 00:18:48.300
let you capture custom metrics.

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:50.630
These are often business
metrics or things like that.

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:52.640
For this e-commerce
system, what we've captured

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:56.870
is the number of items purchased
in a particular checkout

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:57.620
action.

00:18:57.620 --> 00:19:00.470
And so this is captured
over a one-minute time

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:02.886
interval, and then
[AUDIO OUT] after that.

00:19:02.886 --> 00:19:04.760
And we can see over
time, the number of items

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:07.070
purchased while we
run our load test.

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:08.750
The various numbers
of items purchased

00:19:08.750 --> 00:19:11.690
ranges between one item and
six items per checkout action

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:12.897
over a minute.

00:19:12.897 --> 00:19:14.480
And so this is
obviously not something

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:16.188
that you could ever
capture automatically

00:19:16.188 --> 00:19:17.365
from your application.

00:19:17.365 --> 00:19:18.740
Business metrics
like this always

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:19.990
have to be custom defined.

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:23.440
But OpenCensus also
makes that fairly easy.

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:25.855
So if we go above,
we can see here,

00:19:25.855 --> 00:19:27.730
this is where we've
actually gone and defined

00:19:27.730 --> 00:19:29.320
the custom view.

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:31.270
So while, of course,
it's a little more work

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:33.228
than the things that we
automatically capture--

00:19:33.228 --> 00:19:36.220
that's the benefit of
them being automatic--

00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:38.650
it's still very, very simple
to define and register

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:40.909
a custom view inside of
your application's code.

00:19:40.909 --> 00:19:43.450
And again, the nice thing about
this being in your app's code

00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:45.090
is that it's defined
in source control.

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:47.380
If you need to make changes,
version it, roll it back,

00:19:47.380 --> 00:19:49.730
you already have
processes around that.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:53.222
And so doing things like
registering a new view--

00:19:53.222 --> 00:19:54.930
you can simply put
that in a code review,

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:57.280
push that out to production,
data starts showing up.

00:19:57.280 --> 00:19:57.850
It's really powerful.

00:19:57.850 --> 00:19:58.510
BRANDON KVARDE:
And there's nothing

00:19:58.510 --> 00:20:00.340
you have to do on
the back end side?

00:20:00.340 --> 00:20:00.720
MORGAN MCLEAN: That's correct.

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:00.920
BRANDON KVARDE: OK.

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:02.920
MORGAN MCLEAN: We simply started
sending this to our back end--

00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:05.170
in this case Stackdriver,
but that could be Prometheus

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:06.730
or something else--

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:09.040
and nothing there.

00:20:09.040 --> 00:20:11.440
It will just start picking
up this new statistic

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:13.300
and then rendering it to the UI.

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:15.460
So this is where we actually
register the new view.

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:19.294
We can also hop down in the code
to where we actually record it.

00:20:19.294 --> 00:20:21.460
And so this is what you'll
have to write more often.

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:22.012
Right?

00:20:22.012 --> 00:20:24.220
So you typically only need
to register something once

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:25.219
in a particular service.

00:20:25.219 --> 00:20:27.640
But if you want to record
it in multiple locations,

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:29.397
the recording process
is really easy.

00:20:29.397 --> 00:20:31.480
All we do is look at the
length of the order items

00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:33.910
array in our code
and record that down

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:35.160
to the Items Purchased view.

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:36.290
It's really, really simple.

00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:37.840
And that generates
this really nice graph

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:40.090
inside of Stackdriver, or
whatever monitoring solution

00:20:40.090 --> 00:20:41.180
we're using.

00:20:41.180 --> 00:20:42.670
And so with
OpenCensus, we've taken

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:45.370
something that historically is
very difficult-- automatically

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:48.660
capturing traces, propagating
context, attaching metadata

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:51.742
to those traces, in
addition to all the stuff

00:20:51.742 --> 00:20:53.200
on the metric side--
and we've made

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:54.520
it really simple,
and straightforward,

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:55.930
and manageable,
through source control.

00:20:55.930 --> 00:20:57.846
That's why we're so
excited about the project.

00:20:57.846 --> 00:20:59.816
That's why we're talking
about it here today.

00:20:59.816 --> 00:21:01.815
So with that, let's hop
back to our presentation

00:21:01.815 --> 00:21:02.880
and finish things up.

00:21:07.396 --> 00:21:09.770
So there are also some really
cool features in OpenCensus

00:21:09.770 --> 00:21:11.430
that I didn't show you today.

00:21:11.430 --> 00:21:13.830
One of those is Z-pages.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:16.940
So Z-pages are these
health or status pages

00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:19.370
that you can optionally
host from the OpenCensus

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:21.380
clients themselves.

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:23.135
What they show you
is the firehose view

00:21:23.135 --> 00:21:25.520
of stats and RPC metadata.

00:21:25.520 --> 00:21:28.252
This is information that's not
typically sent to a back end.

00:21:28.252 --> 00:21:29.960
If you think of stats
sent to a back end,

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:32.418
they're usually aggregated over
a minute or some other time

00:21:32.418 --> 00:21:34.880
period to save space and
reduce network congestion.

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:37.310
And traces, as I mentioned
before, are typically sampled.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:40.966
Most companies sample
at a roughly 1% rate.

00:21:40.966 --> 00:21:42.590
So the Z-pages show
you the information

00:21:42.590 --> 00:21:44.972
that's not always sent to
your back end for analysis.

00:21:44.972 --> 00:21:46.430
This is useful if
you think there's

00:21:46.430 --> 00:21:47.900
an issue with a
particular service

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:49.610
or a particular
instance of a service,

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:51.170
but it's not being
captured by your back end.

00:21:51.170 --> 00:21:52.310
You can jump into the Z-page.

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:54.684
You can see everything that's
running through that system

00:21:54.684 --> 00:21:55.812
and catch any patterns.

00:21:55.812 --> 00:21:57.770
We're also building some
interaction mechanisms

00:21:57.770 --> 00:22:00.260
between Z-pages and the
OpenCensus clients where

00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:03.400
you can do things like
change the sampling strategy

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:05.930
inside of a Z-page without
having to redeploy your app.

00:22:05.930 --> 00:22:07.490
We use these at
Google extensively.

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:09.448
We're actually really
excited to see how people

00:22:09.448 --> 00:22:10.800
outside of Google use them.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:14.850
The next thing we have is
the W3C trace-context format.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:18.480
So about two years ago, we
kicked off a small project

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:20.470
between Google and
some Zipkin developers

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:23.460
to define a common trace
context propagation format.

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:26.130
That's since grown to involve
a number of other big companies

00:22:26.130 --> 00:22:29.820
like Microsoft,
Dynatrace, and others.

00:22:29.820 --> 00:22:31.700
Context propagation,
as I mentioned before,

00:22:31.700 --> 00:22:35.265
is what allows you to actually
get traces across services.

00:22:35.265 --> 00:22:37.390
It's typically a
common ID that's

00:22:37.390 --> 00:22:40.910
used to link when service A
talks to service B, B to C, C

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:43.070
to D, and so on.

00:22:43.070 --> 00:22:44.632
That context will be the same.

00:22:44.632 --> 00:22:46.340
And that's how, later
on, you can process

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:48.170
those to build a single trace.

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:50.600
The challenge today is
that vendors typically

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:53.180
use their own context
propagation formats.

00:22:53.180 --> 00:22:54.680
So for Stackdriver,
historically, we

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:58.480
had an HTTP header called, I
think, X Google Cloud Trace.

00:22:58.480 --> 00:23:01.010
For Zipkin, they have
various HTTP headers

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:03.590
that are known as the
B3 headers, and so on.

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:05.780
And so there are
a lot of benefits

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:08.530
to having vendors align on
a common context propagation

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:09.155
format.

00:23:09.155 --> 00:23:11.780
One of those is that if you have
multiple vendors participating

00:23:11.780 --> 00:23:14.280
in a trace-- let's say you have
different companies and they

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:15.740
want to trace a
single interaction,

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:18.200
or different parts of a company
use different back ends--

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:20.440
you want those to be
able to communicate.

00:23:20.440 --> 00:23:23.340
The second is black box tracing.

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:25.760
So if you have something
like a network load balancer

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:28.490
inside of your application
that strips headers from HTTP

00:23:28.490 --> 00:23:31.370
requests, you'll break a trace.

00:23:31.370 --> 00:23:34.010
With a common context
propagation format or even

00:23:34.010 --> 00:23:36.320
just a common header, that
won't happen, because now we

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:37.486
can work with those vendors.

00:23:37.486 --> 00:23:40.310
And certainly, big names like
Google, Microsoft, Dynatrace,

00:23:40.310 --> 00:23:42.739
and others, we
can achieve this--

00:23:42.739 --> 00:23:44.780
where we can tell those
vendors of load balancers

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:46.890
and other things to stop
stripping headers or at least

00:23:46.890 --> 00:23:48.431
stop stripping this
particular header

00:23:48.431 --> 00:23:50.745
so that the trace will continue.

00:23:50.745 --> 00:23:52.870
As we discussed before,
another really cool feature

00:23:52.870 --> 00:23:55.550
of OpenCensus is
multi-exporter support.

00:23:55.550 --> 00:23:57.510
So you can choose multiple
exporters at once.

00:23:57.510 --> 00:23:59.595
This is useful in
situations where

00:23:59.595 --> 00:24:01.720
you have different parts
of your organization using

00:24:01.720 --> 00:24:02.650
different back ends.

00:24:02.650 --> 00:24:04.600
Let's say, maybe,
you use a combination

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:08.620
of Zipkin and Prometheus where
you have metrics sent to one,

00:24:08.620 --> 00:24:11.437
stats to the other-- or,
sorry, traces to the other.

00:24:11.437 --> 00:24:12.520
And so we can handle that.

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:13.894
Or if you have--

00:24:13.894 --> 00:24:16.060
let's say you're using
Zipkin in part of the company

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:19.960
and Solarwinds in another part,
or SignalFx in another part.

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:22.942
With OpenCensus, you can
support all those back ends.

00:24:22.942 --> 00:24:25.400
So you don't have to build the
instrumentation three times,

00:24:25.400 --> 00:24:26.770
which is awesome.

00:24:26.770 --> 00:24:29.170
Secondly, you can send all
of the data to all back ends.

00:24:29.170 --> 00:24:30.760
So if one team wants
to use one back end,

00:24:30.760 --> 00:24:32.170
and another team
wants to use another,

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:33.753
you could support
that with OpenCensus

00:24:33.753 --> 00:24:35.050
with very little work.

00:24:35.050 --> 00:24:37.499
And so the multi-exporter
support is great.

00:24:37.499 --> 00:24:40.040
It lets you achieve scenarios
that were really never possible

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:42.035
before without an
extreme amount of work.

00:24:42.035 --> 00:24:43.660
And it also removes
a lot of stickiness

00:24:43.660 --> 00:24:45.070
where, if you do want
to change vendors,

00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:47.060
you can do it with just
changing one line of code,

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:47.600
which is great.

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:49.308
BRANDON KVARDE: Are
there any differences

00:24:49.308 --> 00:24:51.850
in what you can send to
the different exporters

00:24:51.850 --> 00:24:53.287
and back ends?

00:24:53.287 --> 00:24:54.120
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:57.370
The nice thing in this
industry, at least,

00:24:57.370 --> 00:25:00.310
is that generally, most vendors
have similar notions of what

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:02.320
a metric is or what a trace is.

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:05.830
And so generally, if you choose
one vendor versus another,

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:07.996
basically everything is
going to show up either way.

00:25:07.996 --> 00:25:08.871
BRANDON KVARDE: Cool.

00:25:08.871 --> 00:25:10.722
MORGAN MCLEAN: And so
in theory, I guess,

00:25:10.722 --> 00:25:13.180
you could have some vendors
supporting features that others

00:25:13.180 --> 00:25:13.490
don't.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:15.649
But most of them all support
the same feature set.

00:25:15.649 --> 00:25:17.440
And for the OpenCensus
open source project,

00:25:17.440 --> 00:25:20.260
we've been very adamant that
all features need to support

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:22.030
Zipkin and Prometheus.

00:25:22.030 --> 00:25:24.800
So we're not going to add any
vendor-specific special sauce.

00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:27.250
So there's not going to be
any special, Stackdriver-only

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:28.512
features in OpenCensus.

00:25:28.512 --> 00:25:30.220
That would be really
bad for the project.

00:25:30.220 --> 00:25:33.010
Everything has to support
our sanctioned open source

00:25:33.010 --> 00:25:35.687
back end.

00:25:35.687 --> 00:25:38.020
So now, I'm going to talk
about some of the new features

00:25:38.020 --> 00:25:40.060
we're developing for
OpenCensus going forward.

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:42.520
Everything that I've described
thus far is for the project

00:25:42.520 --> 00:25:44.400
as it exists today.

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:47.237
And so we've already come a long
way and had a lot of success,

00:25:47.237 --> 00:25:49.570
but we're doing a lot of work
over the upcoming quarters

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:50.470
and years.

00:25:50.470 --> 00:25:51.692
The first is really simple.

00:25:51.692 --> 00:25:53.650
We're just expanding our
list of integrations--

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:57.040
so getting support for more
client libraries, more storage

00:25:57.040 --> 00:25:58.140
clients, and so on.

00:25:58.140 --> 00:25:59.230
Fairly straightforward.

00:25:59.230 --> 00:26:01.480
The next is this thing called
the OpenCensus collector

00:26:01.480 --> 00:26:02.380
service.

00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:07.345
So today, OpenCensus, just
like the instrumentation

00:26:07.345 --> 00:26:11.927
for various other vendors, sends
data directly to a back end.

00:26:11.927 --> 00:26:14.010
So in that example we had
of service A, service B,

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:16.960
service C, they send data
directly to Stackdriver,

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:19.295
in the demo that we did.

00:26:19.295 --> 00:26:21.420
The collector service will
be an optional component

00:26:21.420 --> 00:26:24.825
that you can put in between
that collects all the traces

00:26:24.825 --> 00:26:26.346
and metrics from
your application

00:26:26.346 --> 00:26:28.470
and then reroutes them to
your back ends of choice.

00:26:28.470 --> 00:26:30.420
It makes exporter
configuration simpler.

00:26:30.420 --> 00:26:32.003
You only have to do
it on the service,

00:26:32.003 --> 00:26:33.210
instead of on each client.

00:26:33.210 --> 00:26:35.160
It also enables things
like smarter sampling.

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:37.950
So instead of randomly
sampling 1% of traces,

00:26:37.950 --> 00:26:40.410
you could sample after
the fact and capture

00:26:40.410 --> 00:26:42.270
traces that have high
latency, for example,

00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:43.650
or traces with
associated errors,

00:26:43.650 --> 00:26:46.230
or some other interesting
characteristics, which is

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:48.090
an incredibly powerful feature.

00:26:48.090 --> 00:26:50.947
That makes tracing
dramatically more effective.

00:26:50.947 --> 00:26:52.780
The other thing it will
enable, longer-term,

00:26:52.780 --> 00:26:53.920
is client app support--

00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:57.250
so support for clients
for iOS, Android, Windows,

00:26:57.250 --> 00:26:59.710
front-end web, and so on--

00:26:59.710 --> 00:27:02.060
where you'll have these
clients send telemetry

00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:04.696
to the OpenCensus service
hosted within your application,

00:27:04.696 --> 00:27:06.070
which then forwards
the data back

00:27:06.070 --> 00:27:07.980
to your back end of choice.

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:11.520
And so that allows you to get
traces to have full and end

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:14.050
view, all the way
from a front-end web

00:27:14.050 --> 00:27:16.995
app to your application
storage layer.

00:27:16.995 --> 00:27:19.330
And so you can see the actual
client-perceived latency,

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:21.038
rather than just your
back end latencies.

00:27:21.038 --> 00:27:22.874
It's really, really great.

00:27:22.874 --> 00:27:25.040
Next, we're going to expand
to more telemetry types.

00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:27.310
So we've been talking
about this for a while,

00:27:27.310 --> 00:27:29.650
but we think we want to
capture things like logs

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:32.820
and exceptions, maybe one
day things like profiles,

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:35.210
but basically more and
more types of telemetry

00:27:35.210 --> 00:27:36.910
from client applications.

00:27:36.910 --> 00:27:38.500
OpenCensus is not
going to be limited

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:40.057
to just metrics and traces.

00:27:40.057 --> 00:27:41.890
That is certainly not
a goal of the project.

00:27:41.890 --> 00:27:43.390
We want to capture
as much as we can

00:27:43.390 --> 00:27:45.155
and send it wherever you want.

00:27:45.155 --> 00:27:47.030
And finally, we want to
keep growing the tent

00:27:47.030 --> 00:27:48.560
to expand new partners.

00:27:48.560 --> 00:27:53.150
So we announced at O'Reilly
Velocity a month or two ago

00:27:53.150 --> 00:27:54.830
that Microsoft has
joined OpenCensus.

00:27:54.830 --> 00:27:57.392
And they're doing the lion's
share of development for .Net.

00:27:57.392 --> 00:27:59.600
They're also making some
pretty sizable contributions

00:27:59.600 --> 00:28:01.670
for Python and,
I think, Node.js.

00:28:01.670 --> 00:28:03.370
And so we're really
excited about that.

00:28:03.370 --> 00:28:06.449
SignalFx has also made and will
be making some contributions.

00:28:06.449 --> 00:28:08.990
And Pivotal, in particular, is
going to be working a lot with

00:28:08.990 --> 00:28:10.832
Microsoft on the
.Net distribution.

00:28:10.832 --> 00:28:12.290
And so we're really
excited to have

00:28:12.290 --> 00:28:15.291
them making big, heavyweight
contributions to the project.

00:28:15.291 --> 00:28:16.790
There are, of course,
also exporters

00:28:16.790 --> 00:28:18.680
supported that
have been developed

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:19.940
by third-party companies.

00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:21.434
So Datadog has
their own exporter,

00:28:21.434 --> 00:28:22.850
SolarWinds has
their own exporter,

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:24.500
and Instana has
their own exporter.

00:28:24.500 --> 00:28:26.377
Finally, the community
has created exporters

00:28:26.377 --> 00:28:27.710
for a number of other back ends.

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:31.220
So for example, we can
send traces to AWS X-Ray--

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:33.410
someone in the community
contributed that--

00:28:33.410 --> 00:28:35.677
similarly, for Jaeger
and other back ends.

00:28:35.677 --> 00:28:37.760
And so we're really excited
to watch the ecosystem

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:38.500
start to grow.

00:28:38.500 --> 00:28:40.080
OpenCensus was
started by Google.

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:42.500
It's historically been an
entirely Google-led project.

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:43.875
We're really
excited to see where

00:28:43.875 --> 00:28:46.731
it's going to go with these
new partners involved.

00:28:46.731 --> 00:28:49.230
And with that, we're done the
main part of the presentation.

00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:50.930
So I think we're going
to take questions.

00:28:50.930 --> 00:28:52.220
We're going to go offline
for a minute or two,

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:53.480
and then we'll start
answering questions.

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:53.930
BRANDON KVARDE: Yep.

00:28:53.930 --> 00:28:54.555
Thanks, Morgan.

00:28:54.555 --> 00:28:56.000
And everybody on
the live stream,

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:58.560
stay tuned for the live Q&amp;A.
We'll be back in less than a

00:28:58.560 --> 00:28:59.060
minute.

00:30:41.320 --> 00:30:42.750
Welcome back, everyone.

00:30:42.750 --> 00:30:45.300
We've received a number of
questions from the audience

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:47.740
that we're going to go
ahead and answer for you.

00:30:47.740 --> 00:30:52.020
So the first one that
we got is, "Is there

00:30:52.020 --> 00:30:54.740
any latency introduced
into the application

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:56.842
when leveraging
OpenCensus for tracing?"

00:30:56.842 --> 00:30:57.675
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:30:57.675 --> 00:30:58.530
That's A good question.

00:30:58.530 --> 00:31:00.280
We get that question
a lot from OpenCensus

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:02.070
or any of the
telemetry recapture.

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:03.570
The answer is,
there's effectively

00:31:03.570 --> 00:31:08.390
no measurable latency introduced
for tracing or for metrics.

00:31:08.390 --> 00:31:12.370
We've done measurements,
it's basically zero.

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:15.060
Keep in mind, tracing
metrics and census impact

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:17.960
are things that we use on all
services running at Google.

00:31:17.960 --> 00:31:21.570
But just for info, you
can stay Google fast,

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:24.600
even while having thousands
of levels of microservices

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:26.440
instrumented with Census.

00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:27.270
Not a problem.

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:28.770
BRANDON KVARDE: So
really no penalty

00:31:28.770 --> 00:31:30.390
to doing that type
of instrumentation?

00:31:30.390 --> 00:31:30.710
MORGAN MCLEAN: No.

00:31:30.710 --> 00:31:31.890
In general, most of these--

00:31:31.890 --> 00:31:34.170
unless you're doing
things like profiling

00:31:34.170 --> 00:31:35.490
where it's constantly on--

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:37.110
in general, most
telemetry collection

00:31:37.110 --> 00:31:38.800
has effectively no
impact on your app.

00:31:38.800 --> 00:31:40.258
It consumes a little
bit of memory,

00:31:40.258 --> 00:31:43.170
maybe like five megs of RAM,
but nothing in the critical path

00:31:43.170 --> 00:31:44.170
for latency.

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:45.180
BRANDON KVARDE: Awesome.

00:31:45.180 --> 00:31:46.440
All right, next question.

00:31:46.440 --> 00:31:50.250
"What makes OpenCensus
different from, say, Grafana

00:31:50.250 --> 00:31:51.630
or other monitoring tools?"

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:52.421
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yes.

00:31:52.421 --> 00:31:54.210
Another question that
we get very often.

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:55.959
So it's really important
to keep in mind--

00:31:55.959 --> 00:31:58.390
OpenCensus is just the
telemetry collection.

00:31:58.390 --> 00:32:00.330
So it's just client
libraries that you

00:32:00.330 --> 00:32:03.630
link to your application to send
telemetry to another back end.

00:32:03.630 --> 00:32:05.370
One of those back
ends might be Grafana.

00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:07.619
One of those back ends might
be Zipkin, or Prometheus,

00:32:07.619 --> 00:32:09.960
or Stackdriver, or so on.

00:32:09.960 --> 00:32:12.180
There are other projects,
like Zipkin or Prometheus,

00:32:12.180 --> 00:32:14.530
for example, which offer the
full, end-to-end solution.

00:32:14.530 --> 00:32:17.280
So Zipkin, for
example, has a back end

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:20.360
for processing, a fairly nice
UI that actually shows you

00:32:20.360 --> 00:32:21.710
your traces that you captured--

00:32:21.710 --> 00:32:23.690
Zipkin is a distributed
tracing system--

00:32:23.690 --> 00:32:26.460
and it also has its
own instrumentation.

00:32:26.460 --> 00:32:28.680
OpenCensus, I think, already
has much more breadth

00:32:28.680 --> 00:32:31.110
of instrumentation than Zipkin's
built-in instrumentation

00:32:31.110 --> 00:32:33.636
from its own project,
but it works with Zipkin.

00:32:33.636 --> 00:32:35.010
And so if you want
to use Zipkin,

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:36.060
you can do that with OpenCensus.

00:32:36.060 --> 00:32:37.143
It's a great way to do it.

00:32:37.143 --> 00:32:38.770
It's what we recommend.

00:32:38.770 --> 00:32:41.285
But yeah, the projects' very
scoped just on instrumentation.

00:32:41.285 --> 00:32:43.460
BRANDON KVARDE: Got it.

00:32:43.460 --> 00:32:45.230
Next question from the audience.

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:49.840
"How does OpenCensus compare to
agent based instrumentation?"

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:50.677
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yes.

00:32:50.677 --> 00:32:52.760
This is actually a pretty
good technical question.

00:32:52.760 --> 00:32:57.410
So OpenCensus, like, I think,
Zipkin and some other tracing

00:32:57.410 --> 00:33:00.290
solutions, captures your
traces from a library

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:02.460
that you link to
your application.

00:33:02.460 --> 00:33:04.344
This has some positives
and negatives.

00:33:04.344 --> 00:33:06.260
So one of the big benefits
that I talked about

00:33:06.260 --> 00:33:08.790
during our presentation
is that you can control it

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:09.980
through source control.

00:33:09.980 --> 00:33:13.100
And so you can manage, through
source control, deployments.

00:33:13.100 --> 00:33:16.340
You can do things like link
OpenCensus to your app,

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:18.190
change its version,
reconfigure it.

00:33:18.190 --> 00:33:20.330
This is great if you're a
developer, because you're

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:22.160
already used to using source
control to make changes

00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:24.610
to your app and redeploy them,
roll them back, and so on.

00:33:24.610 --> 00:33:25.901
And so it's very native to you.

00:33:25.901 --> 00:33:28.790
And you're already using your
languages, built-in package

00:33:28.790 --> 00:33:30.660
management solution,
to get OpenCensus.

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:33.410
So Go, it's like, go get
OpenCensus, for example.

00:33:33.410 --> 00:33:35.870
And so for developers, this
is really native to them.

00:33:35.870 --> 00:33:37.910
The other benefit of
having a library that's

00:33:37.910 --> 00:33:39.285
linked to your
application is you

00:33:39.285 --> 00:33:40.730
can understand how it works.

00:33:40.730 --> 00:33:43.550
All of the interfaces that
OpenCensus consumes or exposes

00:33:43.550 --> 00:33:45.020
are visible to you.

00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:47.710
And so when OpenCensus talks
to gRPC, you, as a developer,

00:33:47.710 --> 00:33:50.274
can be pretty confident that
it's not doing some weird thing

00:33:50.274 --> 00:33:52.190
that you don't understand,
and it's not poorly

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:53.570
impacting the
performance of your app.

00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:55.361
You can also go and
change those interfaces

00:33:55.361 --> 00:33:56.736
if you, for some
reason, need to.

00:33:56.736 --> 00:33:59.069
It's also open source, which
means you can go and modify

00:33:59.069 --> 00:34:01.580
OpenCensus if there's some
kind of critical issues that--

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:05.202
something crops up that other
people can't fix in time.

00:34:05.202 --> 00:34:07.160
Agent-based instrumentation,
on the other hand,

00:34:07.160 --> 00:34:09.590
which some vendors
use, is an executable

00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:12.230
that you have running in
the background on Kubernetes

00:34:12.230 --> 00:34:14.625
or on the VMs that
host your app.

00:34:14.625 --> 00:34:17.124
One of the perceived benefits
of agent based instrumentation

00:34:17.124 --> 00:34:18.710
is it's entirely automatic.

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:20.540
You download and
install this thing.

00:34:20.540 --> 00:34:22.699
You run it, and it
just captures traces.

00:34:22.699 --> 00:34:23.960
It captures metrics.

00:34:23.960 --> 00:34:25.520
It keeps itself updated.

00:34:25.520 --> 00:34:27.889
The challenge is
it's hard to version.

00:34:27.889 --> 00:34:30.260
Agents are typically tied
to one of your VM images

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:32.520
that you build or
something like that.

00:34:32.520 --> 00:34:34.605
Another challenge is
that if, for agents

00:34:34.605 --> 00:34:36.230
that run on, say,
the JVM, for example,

00:34:36.230 --> 00:34:37.550
they do things like
monkey patching

00:34:37.550 --> 00:34:39.260
and a lot of weird
instrumentation tricks

00:34:39.260 --> 00:34:40.909
to get data out of your app.

00:34:40.909 --> 00:34:42.679
This generally works
relatively well.

00:34:42.679 --> 00:34:44.360
But as a developer,
you don't always

00:34:44.360 --> 00:34:47.060
have confidence that it's going
to do everything that you want.

00:34:47.060 --> 00:34:49.796
Or you can never be too
sure of its interactions.

00:34:49.796 --> 00:34:51.920
I'm not trying to bash
agent based instrumentation.

00:34:51.920 --> 00:34:53.960
The automatic
benefits are tangible.

00:34:53.960 --> 00:34:54.771
They're real.

00:34:54.771 --> 00:34:56.270
But we've pretty
deliberately chosen

00:34:56.270 --> 00:34:57.950
to do things in
process, so the library

00:34:57.950 --> 00:34:58.730
that you link to your code.

00:34:58.730 --> 00:35:00.200
We think it's better for
developers, because they

00:35:00.200 --> 00:35:01.460
can better manage it.

00:35:01.460 --> 00:35:03.065
And they can see
how things work.

00:35:03.065 --> 00:35:04.940
Finally, it makes the
instrumentation process

00:35:04.940 --> 00:35:06.240
a little easier.

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:08.720
For example, for gRPC,
we depend on some hooks

00:35:08.720 --> 00:35:11.720
that the gRPC client libraries
expose to OpenCensus.

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:14.240
And we can version those and
be pretty confident they're not

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:15.179
going to change.

00:35:15.179 --> 00:35:16.970
If you're doing agent
based instrumentation

00:35:16.970 --> 00:35:19.572
and trying to do things
like monkey patch the JVM,

00:35:19.572 --> 00:35:21.530
changes in the things
that you're instrumenting

00:35:21.530 --> 00:35:24.630
can break you, even if
they're not technically

00:35:24.630 --> 00:35:25.844
a breaking change.

00:35:25.844 --> 00:35:28.010
And so we're pretty confident
that, in the long run,

00:35:28.010 --> 00:35:30.122
this is a much better
approach for us.

00:35:30.122 --> 00:35:31.080
BRANDON KVARDE: Got it.

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:33.530
Next question.

00:35:33.530 --> 00:35:35.210
"What happens to
my data if there's

00:35:35.210 --> 00:35:38.377
an interruption between
OpenCensus and my backends?

00:35:38.377 --> 00:35:39.210
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:35:39.210 --> 00:35:40.610
Generally, if it's a
short interruption,

00:35:40.610 --> 00:35:41.484
this isn't a problem.

00:35:41.484 --> 00:35:44.176
So this is defined
in each exporter.

00:35:44.176 --> 00:35:46.800
So I can speak for the ones that
we've written for Stackdriver,

00:35:46.800 --> 00:35:47.300
for example.

00:35:47.300 --> 00:35:49.880
But I think most vendors
have taken care of this

00:35:49.880 --> 00:35:51.690
in a similar way that we have.

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:54.890
Typically, the export will do
a bit of buffering and retries.

00:35:54.890 --> 00:35:57.230
If you have an interruption
for a large amount of time,

00:35:57.230 --> 00:35:58.460
some of that data's
going to get dropped,

00:35:58.460 --> 00:36:00.585
because you don't want your
exporter and OpenCensus

00:36:00.585 --> 00:36:02.180
consuming a crazy amount of RAM.

00:36:02.180 --> 00:36:05.450
But in general,
every vendor's going

00:36:05.450 --> 00:36:08.990
to build really good logic
inside of their exporter

00:36:08.990 --> 00:36:10.074
to deal with that.

00:36:10.074 --> 00:36:11.990
We've talked to some
vendors about sharing it,

00:36:11.990 --> 00:36:14.000
but it seems like most of
them have already built this,

00:36:14.000 --> 00:36:15.295
because for their
own instrumentation,

00:36:15.295 --> 00:36:16.860
they already had to
solve this problem.

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:17.818
BRANDON KVARDE: Got it.

00:36:17.818 --> 00:36:19.460
MORGAN MCLEAN: So yeah.

00:36:19.460 --> 00:36:21.460
BRANDON KVARDE: "What
additional telemetry types

00:36:21.460 --> 00:36:23.440
do you want to
support long-term?"

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:25.610
MORGAN MCLEAN: That is also
a really good question.

00:36:25.610 --> 00:36:28.672
So OpenCensus, today,
does tracing and metrics,

00:36:28.672 --> 00:36:30.130
and another thing
that we call tags

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:33.630
that you can use to
link traces and metrics.

00:36:33.630 --> 00:36:36.330
But we have much grander
ambitions long-term.

00:36:36.330 --> 00:36:38.610
So one thing that we're
discussing right now

00:36:38.610 --> 00:36:42.840
with our partners is support
for logging and exceptions.

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:46.420
This is less of a clear-cut
thing for us to do,

00:36:46.420 --> 00:36:50.706
which is why we're spending the
time discussing it as a group.

00:36:50.706 --> 00:36:52.080
For traces and
metric collection,

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:54.430
when we started development
of OpenCensus two years ago,

00:36:54.430 --> 00:36:55.777
there wasn't a lot of prior art.

00:36:55.777 --> 00:36:58.110
There were certainly a few
other products in this field,

00:36:58.110 --> 00:37:01.920
but they either started roughly
around the same time as us--

00:37:01.920 --> 00:37:05.730
whether that's Prometheus
or OpenTracing and others--

00:37:05.730 --> 00:37:07.500
or they didn't suit
some of our needs.

00:37:07.500 --> 00:37:09.240
But if you look
back before that,

00:37:09.240 --> 00:37:11.670
there really wasn't
anything on the market.

00:37:11.670 --> 00:37:14.790
And so it was a
relatively clear decision

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:16.980
to go and develop
things on our own.

00:37:16.980 --> 00:37:19.640
When we look at
things like logging,

00:37:19.640 --> 00:37:22.020
there are frameworks,
like for Java, like Log4j,

00:37:22.020 --> 00:37:25.540
and the native Java logging
framework, and tons of others.

00:37:25.540 --> 00:37:27.030
And so what we're
debating now is

00:37:27.030 --> 00:37:28.530
whether we want to just
integrate with those--

00:37:28.530 --> 00:37:30.029
and to be clear,
we're already doing

00:37:30.029 --> 00:37:32.370
some of that work, where if
you have OpenCensus on Java,

00:37:32.370 --> 00:37:34.920
we automatically add
trace IDs to your logs

00:37:34.920 --> 00:37:36.486
so you can do
those correlations.

00:37:36.486 --> 00:37:37.860
But what we're
really debating is

00:37:37.860 --> 00:37:40.470
whether we want to do native
logging in OpenCensus.

00:37:40.470 --> 00:37:42.785
So there'd be like
an OpenCensus log API

00:37:42.785 --> 00:37:44.160
that you could
use to write logs,

00:37:44.160 --> 00:37:46.327
just like we wrote metrics
and traces in that demo.

00:37:46.327 --> 00:37:48.160
That's something we're
trying to figure out.

00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:50.576
So there's discussions within
Google and certainly with us

00:37:50.576 --> 00:37:52.920
and with partners like Microsoft
and others about how we

00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:54.510
actually want to approach this.

00:37:54.510 --> 00:37:56.640
In particular, we talked
about client applications.

00:37:56.640 --> 00:37:59.470
So we think there's some pretty
big benefits to providing

00:37:59.470 --> 00:38:03.360
a logging and errors API for iOS
and Android and other clients,

00:38:03.360 --> 00:38:05.569
because there's not a lot
of other equivalents there.

00:38:05.569 --> 00:38:07.318
Whether we want to do
that on the back end

00:38:07.318 --> 00:38:08.370
is still under debate.

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:10.550
I suspect we will
do it at the end.

00:38:10.550 --> 00:38:11.850
BRANDON KVARDE: OK.

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:15.250
Looks like we have time
for one last question.

00:38:15.250 --> 00:38:18.410
"What's the history of
the name OpenCensus?"

00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:20.235
MORGAN MCLEAN: That's funny.

00:38:20.235 --> 00:38:21.660
In other presentations
I've done,

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:24.210
like at GCP Next
and other places,

00:38:24.210 --> 00:38:26.260
I show OpenCensus
in the ecosystem,

00:38:26.260 --> 00:38:30.840
and I show how Google
open-sourced Stubby is gRPC,

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:33.322
and Borg became Kubernetes,
and all these open source

00:38:33.322 --> 00:38:35.280
projects from Google that
are these cool names.

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:38.220
Then I show Census became
OpenCensus because, clearly,

00:38:38.220 --> 00:38:40.860
we're not the most creative.

00:38:40.860 --> 00:38:42.990
The name, of course,
stems from Census,

00:38:42.990 --> 00:38:46.060
which is effectively the same
thing used inside of Google.

00:38:46.060 --> 00:38:47.800
So as I mentioned,
it's our library

00:38:47.800 --> 00:38:49.620
to automatically collect
traces and metrics

00:38:49.620 --> 00:38:55.050
and host our Borg status pages,
the Z-pages inside of Google.

00:38:55.050 --> 00:38:59.115
How Census got its name is
not entirely clear to me.

00:38:59.115 --> 00:39:00.700
It long predates
my time at Google.

00:39:00.700 --> 00:39:04.380
I suspect it's
because a census is

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:07.660
when you have a survey
of many, many entrants

00:39:07.660 --> 00:39:08.910
or many, many participants.

00:39:08.910 --> 00:39:11.230
And given the number of
machines and processes

00:39:11.230 --> 00:39:14.479
we have running at Google, it
seems somewhat appropriate.

00:39:14.479 --> 00:39:16.020
And when we wanted
to open source it,

00:39:16.020 --> 00:39:18.920
the team chose that they
wanted to call it OpenCensus.

00:39:18.920 --> 00:39:20.170
BRANDON KVARDE: Very creative.

00:39:20.170 --> 00:39:22.295
MORGAN MCLEAN: So not the
most exciting of stories,

00:39:22.295 --> 00:39:24.230
but that's how the
project got its name.

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:25.353
BRANDON KVARDE: All right.

00:39:25.353 --> 00:39:26.200
MORGAN MCLEAN: Yeah.

00:39:26.200 --> 00:39:28.015
BRANDON KVARDE:
Well thanks, Morgan,

00:39:28.015 --> 00:39:29.265
for answering those questions.

00:39:29.265 --> 00:39:30.330
MORGAN MCLEAN:
You're most welcome.

00:39:30.330 --> 00:39:32.440
BRANDON KVARDE: Thank you,
everybody, for tuning in today.

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:34.140
Stay tuned for the
next session-- stream

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:37.240
processing with Cloud data
flow, SDKs, and architectures.

00:39:37.240 --> 00:39:39.090
Thank you.

